1. Copy-first rule
•
Every new version starts from the full actual prior file.
•
No rewrite-from-memory.
•
No summary-based rebuild.
•
Old file is copied verbatim first, then edited.
2. Audit stays on inside patch mode
•
Patch mode does not replace audit mode.
•
Every patch is still checked line by line against:
•
actual file
•
v6.8 system rules
•
downstream consumers
3. No ambiguous patch rule
•
If a rule has more than one plausible implementation, I do not patch it as fact.
•
It goes to clarification bucket.
•
I will not “choose” a meaning unless the file + system clearly support it.
4. Root-bug counting only
•
I count the underlying defect once.
•
I do not inflate totals with downstream effects.
•
Downstream effects will still be named, but not double counted.
5. No conservative nulling unless system explicitly allows it
•
I will not turn valid operative logic into None just to avoid a false positive.
•
Null is only allowed when:
•
system explicitly allows null
•
field is manual-by-design
•
provider stack genuinely cannot supply the required concept
6. Provider-concept integrity rule
•
Wrong concept fallback is forbidden.
•
If a field requires one concept, I will not silently substitute a different concept.
7. Provider-specific normalization rule
•
If providers express the same concept differently, I normalize by provider.
•
I do not “fix” that by deleting fields globally.
8. One bug, one exact target behavior
•
Before patching any bug, I must state:
•
exact current behavior
•
•
exact required behavior
exact downstream fields/functions affected
